Now, suppose that $c_j=y_j^1 \vee y_j^2 \vee y_j^3$, where $y_j^k$ is a literal in the clause $c_j$. For such clause $c_j$, we construct a satisfaction testing component $Y_j=(V_j',E_j')$, with $V_j'=\{c_j^1,\ldots, c_j^{10}\}$ and $E_j'=\{c_j^1c_j^2,c_j^1c_j^3,c_j^4c_j^2,c_j^4c_j^3,c_j^2c_j^5,c_j^5c_j^6,c_j^5c_j^7,c_j^3c_j^8,c_j^8c_j^9,c_j^8c_j^{10}\}$ (see Figure \ref{figure:NP2} for reference). The component is attached to the rest of the graph only through vertices $c_j^1$ and $c_j^2$ which give us the following claim.

\begin{claim}\label{remark:NP2}
Let $c_j$ be an arbitrary clause in $C$. Any edge metric generator must contain at least one of the vertices $\{c_j^6,c_j^7\}$ and at least one of the vertices $\{c_j^9,c_j^{10}\}$.
\end{claim}

\begin{proof}
Suppose that there exists an edge metric generator $S$ without any of the vertices $\{c_j^6,c_j^7\}$ in it. Since all the shortest paths from any vertex $x\ne c_j^6,c_j^7$ to the edges $c_j^5c_j^6$ and $c_j^5c_j^7$ go through the vertex $c_j^5$, this implies that the edges $c_j^5c_j^6, c_j^5c_j^7$ have the same distance to all vertices in the set $S$, a contradiction. A similar process works for the vertices $\{c_j^9,c_j^{10}\}$.
\end{proof}

