159145:# fails.  What is lost is the paper's mathematics, before the solver sees
159146:# anything.  Observed live: an equiangular-lines paper (Gram matrices, Seidel
159147:# matrices, interlacing) shipped as a 720-vertex graph whose verify() is
159148:# documented "check any size-k clique" -- no vectors in the instance at all.
159159:# escalate() decides whether a paper lives: when the oracle solves a level the
159160:# harness climbs the ladder, and a one-dial ladder that only raises n runs into the
159161:# output cap and returns None, which the harness reports as too_easy. 33 of 54
159162:# shipped modules moved exactly one axis. Require a second.
159196:# Read what the SOLVER is actually shown, not the dict key names -- renaming
159197:# "edges" to "pairs" was enough to evade this entirely.  Mirrored in
159198:# scripts/corpus_report.py; the gate and the report must never disagree.
159200:# These are WORD-BOUNDED regexes, not substrings.  Plain substring "graph" matched
159201:# "crypto-graph-ic" and blocked 1912.02640, a finite-field polynomial_identity
159202:# module, on the word "cryptographic" in its own problem statement -- and would
159203:# have blocked every crypto paper that ever says so, which is exactly the
159204:# under-represented family this corpus needs.  "degree of" matched "degree of the
159205:# polynomial" for the same reason and is now pinned to a graph noun.
159225:# The old guard was NAT["domain"] in CONTINUOUS -- unreachable, because the prompt
159226:# tells builders to set domain by what the SOLVER reasons about, so an honest
159227:# geometry->graph reduction declares domain="combinatorics" and the gate never fired.
159228:# Measured: 0 of 7 NATIVE modules could ever trip it.  Guard on the paper's own arXiv
159229:# categories instead; the builder does not choose those.
159259:# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
159260:# canonical_key must be a function of the instance, not of the call.  A key that
159261:# is not deterministic cannot detect a duplicate; we cannot check the harder
159262:# property (invariance under relabelling) without family-specific machinery, so
159263:# the README caveats have to carry that one.
159279:# emit.sh already resamples past duplicates; this re-checks the file it produced,
159280:# so the thing that generates the instances is not the only thing that vouches
159281:# for them.
